  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "None; full paper text is present.",
    "relation": "discusses",
    "tranche": "T6",
    "tranche_reason": "The paper studies fine-tuning and harmful-content refusal, but does not test any listed version: its harmful-generation evaluation uses explicitly harmful instructions, not neutral unrelated prompts after narrow harmful-task fine-tuning.",
    "gate_note": "The safety experiment reports answer/refusal outcomes for explicitly harmful instructions. This is a related finding, but the harmful behavior is elicited by the user prompt, so it does not establish misalignment on unrelated neutral prompts. The paper also reports ICL and natural-language reasoning results, which do not measure the behavior named in the claim versions.",
    "test_reason": "The study is about catastrophic forgetting explained as shifted task inference, and about recovering suppressed pretrained abilities through translated prompts ('conjugate prompting'). Its only safety result compares refusal of explicitly harmful AdvBench requests before and after safety/RLHF tuning. That fine-tuning reduces harmful answering, which is the opposite direction from the claim. There is no narrow harmful fine-tune, no neutral-question misalignment evaluation, no in-context or RL induction of broad misalignment, no mechanism analysis, and no inoculation. It neither tests nor uses any version.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Largely right, but too narrow. The authors conclude that fine-tuning's off-distribution costs, including the suppression of in-context learning, reasoning and harmful-instruction following, mostly reflect shifted implicit task inference rather than lost capability. Translating prompts away from the English fine-tuning distribution partially recovers those behaviours, which also shows safety tuning is brittle.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "The study evaluates safety fine-tuning and language-transformed harmful prompts, not narrow harmful-task SFT followed by neutral-prompt misalignment evaluation.",
    "method_sensitivity": "Language of the harmful instruction changes the reported answer frequency; non-English prompts generally show smaller answer-frequency drops than English.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "On 100 explicitly harmful AdvBench instructions per language, the safety-tuned chat model answers far less than text-davinci-003 in English (3% vs 92%). The gap shrinks in Japanese, Hungarian and Swahili, and nearly vanishes in Malayalam (65% vs 71%).",
        "proposed_claim": "Safety fine-tuning suppresses harmful answering mainly in the fine-tuning language; translating prompts partially recovers compliance.",
